Lodestone
Preliminary Proposal


Team 16: Jack Ngo(CE), Owen Klobucher(CE), Wil Dems(CE), Quinn Carolan(CE), Eric Tang(CE)

Project Idea: 
Our current idea is Lodestone. A low-cost and long-battery life wearable that logs your path with GNSS, 
shares it with teammates over LoRa, and feeds a laptop app that shows the whole group’s trails and last known positions.
No cell service, subscription, or phone required. 

Problem Statement: 
Cell service is often unavailable in remote hiking and camping areas, leaving separated group members unable to see each other’s locations and without a last known position if someone fails to return. 

Pre-existing solutions and what your project will do differently when solving the problem: 
Totem Compass: 
- Wearable that points to friends with colored lights
- No phone, Wifi, phone, or cell service required
- GNSS used for positioning, and 1 km direct range in open conditions
- About 10-24 hours of battery life depending on the mode
- No display, map, or messaging
- Direction lights only
- Short battery life. 
- Less than 1 day of battery life
- Limited use and range
- Main use is for concerts
LynQ
- Digital compass that points towards friends
- No cell or WiFi needed
- Claimed roughly 3 miles of range
- Small round monochrome screen
- Carabiner Style body with a locking clip 
- About 24 hours of active use
- Display is minimal
- Only shows direction and distance of one target
-Unreliable in dense environments
- User reports claim GPS signal issues, especially in densely wooded areas. 
- Long link time
- Users report up to 5 min wait times when initializing two Lynq’s


Subscription dependency: Existing mainstream devices from companies like Garmin and Fitbit require a subscription plan to utilize 
                          their service to the fullest extent. Our’s won’t require a subscription or license 
Network dependency: most existing devices utilize cellular or Wi-Fi, which can be unreliable for hikers and campers in remote areas. 
                      Ours will utilize LoRa and GNSS
Cost: Most of these devices range from as low as $120 per unit, to upwards of $1600 + subscription costs. Ours will aim to be around 
      $100 per unit, as a high limit. 


Expected difficulties
- Increasing battery life
- Fitting antenna on wrist
- Directional navigation
- Configuring LoRa and mesh network 

